  <title>Move from GNOME Terminal</title>

<p><app>Pano Terminal</app> keeps its own profiles and preferences, so that it
  can be installed next to <app>GNOME Terminal</app> without changing it.</p>

  <section id="import">
    <title>Import the settings of GNOME Terminal</title>

    <p>When <app>Pano Terminal</app> starts for the first time and finds
    settings of <app>GNOME Terminal</app>, it asks whether to import your
    profiles, keyboard shortcuts and preferences. Click
    <gui style="button">Import</gui> to copy them.</p>

    <p>To import them later:</p>
    <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Application</gui> group, click <gui style="button">Import</gui> next to <gui>Import from GNOME Terminal</gui>.</p></item>
</steps>

    <p>The profiles are added to those you already have. A profile that both
    have, such as the default profile they both start with, is replaced, and
    the default profile of <app>GNOME Terminal</app> becomes the default.
    Your keyboard shortcuts and preferences are replaced by those of
    <app>GNOME Terminal</app>. The settings of <app>GNOME Terminal</app> are
    not changed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="default">
    <title>Make Pano Terminal the default terminal</title>

    <p>The default terminal is the one that the desktop and other applications,
    such as the file manager, open. <app>Pano Terminal</app> asks whether it
    should be the default when it is not. To make it the default:</p>
    <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Application</gui> group, click <gui style="button">Set as Default Terminal</gui>.</p></item>
</steps>

    <p>Turn off <gui>Ask to Remain Default</gui> in the same group if you do
    not want to be asked again.</p>
  </section>

